Deafness
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Absent startle reflex, Delayed speech development, Reliance on visual/gestural communication

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-015
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral crisis, severe agitation, and minor self-inflicted trauma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 4-year-old child with profound sensorineural hearing loss exhibiting uncontrollable, severe frustration, throwing heavy objects, and head-banging against the floor for 2 hours. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of severe traumatic brain injury from the head-banging and the need to rule out an occult physical injury or illness causing the sudden behavioral decompensation in an aggressively non-verbal child.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He is completely unreachable, screaming, smashing his head on the tiles, and we cannot figure out what is wrong.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 2-hour history of acute behavioral crisis and severe agitation. The patient has a known history of profound deafness characterized by an absent startle reflex since birth and completely delayed speech development. He relies entirely on visual/gestural communication. Today, the patient became increasingly frustrated when his tablet (used for visual communication apps) broke. Unable to articulate his needs, he developed severe social withdrawal, which escalated into violent frustration, throwing objects, and recurrent head-banging. The parents note he has been pulling at his left ear aggressively.
Functional Status: Ambulant; dependent on parents for translation and daily living needs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of serious head injury; severe communication barrier preventing de-escalation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 38.2 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 18.0 Kg
General: Child is extremely distressed, sweating, and actively resisting examination, emitting loud, non-articulated vocalizations.
Physical Exam: Physical exam reveals an abrasion and mild hematoma on the forehead from head-banging. Neurological exam is grossly normal, with no focal motor deficits; pupillary reflexes are brisk and equal. Otoscopic examination of the right ear is normal. Otoscopic examination of the left ear reveals a severely bulging, intensely erythematous tympanic membrane with a visible purulent effusion behind it. Throat is clear. Abdomen is soft and non-tender.
Environmental Observations: Broken electronic tablet on the table; the room lacks adequate low-stimulation sensory areas.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. No analgesia given by parents due to inability to calm the child enough to administer oral liquids.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Deafness (complicated by acute left otitis media and secondary behavioral crisis) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Acute Closed Head Injury - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the agitation, because the agitation preceded the head-banging, and there are no signs of raised intracranial pressure (e.g., unequal pupils, vomiting).

Dental Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to visual inspection showing intact dentition without gingival swelling, and localization of pulling to the ear rather than the jaw.

Autism Spectrum Disorder (Acute sensory overload) - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole cause, because the objective finding of a severe, acute, painful physical infection (otitis media) fully explains the sudden escalation in a child who cannot verbalize localized pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of intractable head-banging and severe agitation represented a crisis of unmet needs due to profound communication barriers. The objective discovery of a high fever (38.2 C) and a severely bulging, inflamed tympanic membrane confirmed that the behavioral explosion was a direct manifestation of severe, uncommunicated physical pain. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because the child was at immediate risk of self-inflicting a severe traumatic brain injury, and urgent pharmacological pain relief and antibiotic therapy were mandated to resolve the underlying physical trigger.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a weight-based dose of Ibuprofen rectally (suppository) due to total refusal of oral intake, providing rapid analgesia. Prescribed Amoxicillin/Clavulanate oral suspension to treat the severe otitis media. Cleaned and dressed the superficial forehead abrasion.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you use a standardized picture-exchange card for "pain" and point to body parts, you give him a voice; therefore, practicing this specific card daily when he is well builds a critical communication bridge for future illnesses. When you immediately provide a soft crash-mat during meltdowns, you protect his head; placing this mat in his room creates a safe space that minimizes injury risk.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to speech pathology to integrate physical symptom vocabulary into his visual communication strategy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended purchasing a heavy-duty protective case for the replacement communication tablet.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours to assess the tympanic membrane and behavioral baseline.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the child becomes unusually lethargic and difficult to wake, if he vomits repeatedly, or if fluid or blood begins to drain from the ear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Behavioral crises in non-verbal patients must always be treated as a medical event until a physical cause is ruled out.
Clinical Course: The rectal ibuprofen will reduce the ear pain within 45 minutes, leading to a rapid de-escalation of the behavioral crisis. The ear infection will resolve over 5-7 days with antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical review if the high fever persists beyond 48 hours of antibiotic therapy, or if the severe head-banging resumes despite regular pain medication.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: His severe behavior was his only way of communicating the intense pain from the ear infection. The suppository bypassed his refusal to swallow, treating the pain directly. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria in the ear.
Actionable Steps: Administer regular pain relief for the next 48 hours. Give the antibiotics exactly as charted. Introduce a "pain" visual communication card.

